-- chem/gaugetest.cub — decided in the small universe: does the exclusion gauge repair the pair bound? The universe is
-- chem/fock.cub's: hydrogen's 1s and 2s at Z = 3, each with spin up and down, modes 0..3 (0, 1 the 1s; 2, 3 the 2s).
-- Lithium is three particles there; its lowest level with one spin down is in [−7444750, −7444749]/2²⁰ (−7.09987).
-- The pair operator, in the atom's units, for N = 3:
--   K_c = ½(h₁ + h₂) + V − (c/2)(P₁ + P₂) + 2c·P₁P₂,   P the projector on the spatial 1s orbital,
-- which on unnormalised orthogonal modes with norms d_p has one-particle entries ½h_pq − (c/2)d_p (p = q a 1s mode,
-- ½h_pq otherwise) and two-particle entries v(p,q,r,s) + 2c·d_p·d_q (p = r and q = s both 1s modes, v otherwise)
-- (`gt-h`, `gt-v`). e_s(c) is its lowest level with one spin down (the singlet ground level lies there, below the
-- triplet), e_t(c) its level with no spin down (one state, (1s↑, 2s↑): an exact rational, `gt-et`). The bound is
--   B(c) = (3/2)·e_s(c) + (3/2)·e_t(c),
-- chem/pairgauge.cub's `pg-bound` at N = 3, S = ½ in the atom's units (`gt-pg`). Taken, not proved here: the pair
-- decomposition's bound itself (chem/pairgauge.cub's statement), inside this universe as outside it. The searches
-- (`gt-find`) are outside every proof; the results are in the lines added below the definitions.
